{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And he sprinkled from it upon the altar</b>. Rashi writes (Rashi on Leviticus 8:11), \"And I do not know where he was commanded concerning these sprinklings.\" But it is possible that what is said concerning the altar (v. 10) — \"and you shall consecrate the altar, and it shall be most holy\" — means that he is to add this consecration to it by sprinkling, and it is not by anointing alone. For \"and it shall be most holy\" means that an additional consecration is added to it beyond that of all the other things anointed in the Tabernacle and all its vessels, and he performed for it a consecration like the consecration he was commanded to perform for the priests who offer, as it says (Leviticus 29:21), \"and you shall sprinkle upon Aaron and upon his garments\" — for the altar, which is a vessel for the offerings, is no less than the vessels of those who offer, and all the more so by an a fortiori argument. So too it is said concerning Yom HaKippurim (Leviticus 16:19), \"and he shall sprinkle upon it from the blood with his finger seven times, and cleanse it and consecrate it from the impurities of the children of Israel\" — for all consecration of the altar is by sprinkling, and all the more so at the beginning, when it requires consecration from impurity and from alienness. And he did not mention the anointing of his sons here, for it is known that he did to them as he did to their father, as he was commanded (Exodus 40:15), \"and you shall anoint them as you anointed their father,\" and this was included in what is said concerning the sons (Leviticus 8:13), \"as YHWH commanded Moses.\" Now, what seems right in my eyes is that the anointing of the sons was not by pouring oil on their heads, for that is said only concerning Aaron: \"and you shall pour on his head and anoint him\" (Exodus 29:7), and there it does not mention the anointing of the sons at all, for theirs was not a pouring like his. And it is possible that there was no anointing of the sons other than the sprinkling — that he sprinkled of the anointing oil upon his sons and upon their garments. So too you will see in the passage \"and this is the thing that you shall do to them to consecrate them\" (Exodus 29:1), where he was commanded to anoint the father — \"and you shall take the anointing oil and pour it on his head\" — but was not commanded to anoint the sons at all, neither by pouring nor by anointing. However, in the passage of the erection of the Tabernacle (Exodus 40:15) it says, \"and you shall anoint them as you anointed their father,\" without mentioning sprinkling at all, and likewise in the passage of the oil (Exodus 30:30), and neither anointing nor sprinkling for the sons is mentioned in any single place together. Therefore I say that he did only one of the two for them, namely the sprinkling."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Rashi's Hebrew verbatim including the first-person phrasing; rendered accordingly. The phrase מִטֻּמְאָה וְזָרוּת (\"from impurity and from alienness\") is left literal; זָרוּת may refer to the altar's prior status as unconsecrated or to the prohibition of alien (non-priestly) service, but is translated without resolving the ambiguity."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:53.450697091Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}